A||||6||Shaun41178||Max lift of valvesprings on 3100 heads???||||1||
Z||000000||Shaun41178||12-04-2001||08:46 PM||||just what the topic says.<P>I need to know how much lift the stock valvesprings can take on a gen 3 3100 head with stamped steel rockers arms.  The engine did come with a roller cam so the springs are strong enough for a roller.  I just need to know how big I can go before I reach coilbind.  Also does the 3100 heads have 1.5 or 1.6 rockers?<P>Someone has to know, or know where I can get this info.  I have already posted on other forums with no results yet.  I tried the GP forum and the grand am forum.  Noone seems to know<P>And if these springs aren't strong enough to handle the cam I want, is there an aftermarket company that makes stiffer springs for the gen 3 head without having to modify the spring seat?  I checked Crane Cams catalog and they don't list anything except for the iron head application.  Unless those can be used on gen 3 heads as well?  Anyone, anyone??  Bueller?? Bueller??<P>------------------<BR>Sold Nitrous 85 GT.<BR>Looking for another.<BR>FOR SALE: Aluminum v6 block.<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||12-05-2001||01:31 AM||||The 3100 has standard springs, right?  The 3400 are tapered--a whole other deal...but the roller springs seem to use LESS pressure.<BR>I don't think you'd want to go too heavy, it is aluminum!<P>Anyway, you'll have to measure the pocket.  Crane may have something.  They almost had something for me...until I realized the grooves on the valve were not square but round..the locks would not work!  So, I went another route.<P>The coil bind is measurable...but I forget what the minimum space between coils should be.<P>The 3100 could possible have 1.6 rockers...call up GM and ask them to look in the specs section of their manual for the car it came out of.  Its in there-- including spring specs.<P><BR>Hey...you're selling your aluminum block? What's the deal?<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shaun41178||12-05-2001||04:23 PM||||Joe p[lease explain to me what you mean by tapered springs.  I have never heard that expression before when referring to springs.  I am guessing you can not tell by the pics I sent you?<P>I actually believe the roller springs use more pressure(stiffer) because of the steeper ramp profiles.  I could be wrong on this but I am pretty sure I am right.<P>I am pretty sure I could get away with some pretty stiff springs if I have to.  The aluminum should be thick enough in the spring pocket to allow stiffer springs.  I know that GM used a cam with .560 lift in the 90 beretta pace car engine. This was gen 2 heads and not a roller cam but it shows that with the amount of lift that cam produced it needed some pretty heave duty springs to return the valves to closing and to follow the cam profile and also to stay away from coilbind cause no way the stockers could take that much lift.<P>Yes I know how to measure coilbind.  I have the forumula here.  Spacing is supposed to be  around the thickness of a small paperclip at max lift.  Roughly anyway.<P>When you say the grooves on the valve were round do you mean the groove where the locks go to hold the retainers and springs to the valve?  if so then these grooves are supposed to be round.  I have not seen a valve where the groove was square cause it is my assumption that you have to allow the valve to rotate a bit to help keep crap from building up on it and not seeling properly.<P>Alright I guess I will call a GM dealership.  They could probably find out for me after having me on hold for a good 4-5 hours.  hehe.<P>Yea well I am looking to sell everything really.  If I don't sell it then no biggie as I will hang onto it but if a buyer comes along then it is out of here.  No point in having it around if I have nothing to put it in.  Still looking but havn't found anything that good yet.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||12-05-2001||04:37 PM||||Tapered springs have a variable spring diameter.  Imagine a cone shaped spring; I don't know what the advantage is of this design..some cars use them, some don't.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Joe Torma||12-05-2001||08:35 PM||||Shaun,<P>Bryce is right.  The diameter of the 3400 springs are smaller at the top than the bottom.  The advantage?  No damper spring is necessary.<P>The closed seat pressure is 75lbs for the 3400...not very much.  Good for 6000rpm though....7000 with modification.<P>Look at the groove cut around the valve.  The 2.8s and every other one has a square groove...the 3400 has round.<P>If you sell everything....what are you going to build?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||12-05-2001||10:07 PM||||The complete specs for the springs including open, closed, bind, and free heights, and force to compress should be on the engine specifications page of the GM manual.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shaun41178||12-07-2001||09:37 AM||||Thanks rockcrawl.  I unfortuneatly don't have one of those books but I am sure that a GM dealership will.<P>I will try to find out what it is within the next couple of days and let the forum know.||||reg||1
